C.push(['affil', '*Corresponding author: niranjanl1983@gmail.com; co-author: kr.chaithra@gmail.com']);
const mr = covRange(CM.groups, 'cov_pos');
C.push(['abstract', `Explainable cardiovascular risk tools usually explain a black-box model after the fact, so the model, its explanation and the narrative shown to the clinician can disagree. This paper proposes an interpretable-by-design framework in which every explanation is exact and every sentence of the risk narrative can be re-derived from the model. The framework combines a guideline-anchored monotone additive model (GAMA), whose risk curves have knots at clinical cut-points for blood pressure, cholesterol and glucose and cannot run against established risk directions; a certified narrative compiler, which attaches a checkable certificate (value, band, contribution, odds multiplier, rank and source) to every statement, with an independent verifier; an exact minimum-cost counterfactual search; and subgroup-conditional (Mondrian) conformal screening. On the public Framingham dataset (${N} participants, ${A.events} ten-year coronary events) under repeated nested cross-validation, GAMA reached a ROC-AUC of ${auc('GAMA')} and ranked third of fourteen systems. It was statistically tied with logistic regression, an explainable boosting machine and extra trees, and significantly better than the other ten classifiers, including XGBoost and LightGBM. It showed no monotonicity violations (LightGBM: ${pc(VI.LightGBM.rate / 100)}%) and the most stable attributions (Spearman ρ ${f(ST.GAMA.rho)}). The counterfactual search was never undercut by random or greedy search. The verifier blocked ${VT.full.toLocaleString('en-US')} of ${VT.n.toLocaleString('en-US')} injected narrative errors, against ${pc(VT.bind / VT.n)}% for value–concept binding, without false alarms. Conformal screening held coverage among coronary events at ${pc(mr[0])}–${pc(mr[1])}% in every sex and age subgroup, versus ${pc(CMg.cov_pos)}% for marginal conformal prediction. The complete pipeline ran in a median of ${f(RT.negative.total.median, 0)}–${f(RT.positive.total.median, 0)} ms per patient. Exact, verifiable explanation therefore cost no discrimination; clinical value requires external and prospective validation.`]);
C.push(['keywords', 'Explainable artificial intelligence; Cardiovascular risk prediction; Monotone additive model; Counterfactual explanation; Conformal prediction']);

// ================= I. INTRODUCTION =================
h1('1. Introduction');
p('Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death worldwide {who_cvd}, and coronary heart disease (CHD) accounts for a large share of that burden. Most of it is preventable: blood pressure, cholesterol, glucose, body weight and smoking can all be modified, and treatment decisions in primary prevention are therefore driven by an estimate of a person’s absolute risk over the next ten years. For more than two decades this estimate has come from transparent epidemiological equations. The Framingham general cardiovascular risk profile {dagostino2008} and the Pooled Cohort Equations {goff2014} remain in use not because they are the most accurate models available, but because every component can be read, checked against the patient’s chart, discussed with the patient and documented. A clinician can see why a score is high, and can see which measurements, if improved, would lower it.');
p('Machine learning has been applied extensively to the same problem. Ensembles, gradient-boosted trees and neural networks trained on the public Framingham dataset and similar cohorts frequently report improved retrospective discrimination {talukder2025,adekoya2025,chaudhary2026,raman2026}. These gains come at a price that matters in clinical practice. A boosted ensemble of hundreds of trees has no readable structure, so a clinician cannot tell whether a high score reflects a plausible combination of risk factors or an artefact of the training data. Ackerman’s socio-technical gap {ackerman2000}—the mismatch between how people reason and what a system computes—is acute here: a clinician who cannot see why a model labels a patient high-risk can neither defer to it responsibly nor document a defensible rationale. Regulators have responded by emphasising transparency, traceability and human oversight for machine-learning medical software {fda2021}, and surveys of explainable AI in health care identify explanation quality as a precondition for trust {markus2021,rosenbacke2024}.');
p('The dominant response has been post-hoc explanation. SHAP allocates each prediction among the input features using Shapley values {lundberg2017,lundberg2020}, LIME fits a local linear surrogate around each prediction {ribeiro2016}, and most recent cardiovascular studies report one or both {talukder2025,adekoya2025,raman2026,salih2025}. Post-hoc explainers, however, approximate a model rather than describe it, and the approximation has measurable failure modes. Different explainers often rank features differently for the same prediction {krishna2022}. They can be deliberately deceived by models that behave differently on and off the data manifold {slack2020}. With correlated clinical variables, such as systolic blood pressure, diastolic blood pressure and a hypertension diagnosis, they evaluate the model on implausible patients and the attributions depend on how absent features are filled in {aas2021}. Composite trust scores that summarise agreement between explainers {pal2026,bansal2025} are useful warnings, but agreement between two approximations is not evidence that either is faithful to the model.');
p('A further step has recently been added: large language models are asked to turn predictions and attributions into readable clinical narratives {kapadiya2026}. Fluent prose is easier to use than a vector of attributions, but generated text introduces its own errors—values that were never supplied, correct numbers attached to the wrong measurement, reversed directions of effect and invented citations {ji2023,singhal2023}. In our earlier work {paper1} we addressed this by placing a verification gate between the generator and the reader. That gate bound every value, unit, guideline category and direction in a narrative to a structured payload, and it blocked all of 192 injected factual errors that a numeric check and a semantic-similarity check partly missed. The same study also exposed the limit of the approach. The gate could verify what a narrative said about the patient’s data. It could not verify what the narrative said about the model—how large a contribution was, which factor was the strongest, whether a suggested change would actually alter the prediction, or how certain the screen was—because the explanations being verbalised were themselves approximations of a black-box classifier. In that study most LIME surrogates fitted their own neighbourhoods poorly, even when they agreed with SHAP.');
p('Rudin argued that in high-stakes decisions the better course is to use models that are interpretable in the first place, rather than to explain black boxes after the fact {rudin2019}. For tabular clinical data this argument is strengthened by repeated evidence that carefully regularised simple models discriminate about as well as complex ones. Interpretable additive models already exist {hastie1986,lou2012,caruana2015,nori2019}, and monotonicity constraints are available in several learners {gupta2016,ke2017}, but these strands have not been combined into a system in which the explanation, the narrative, the suggested changes and the uncertainty statement all derive from one model and can all be checked against it. This paper asks whether that is possible: *if the model is additive and constrained by clinical knowledge, can every sentence of a cardiovascular risk narrative carry a proof that a simple program can check, without losing discrimination?* We address four concrete questions. Does an additive model whose structure follows clinical guidelines discriminate and calibrate as well as established machine-learning baselines? Are its explanations more consistent with clinical knowledge and more stable than post-hoc attributions? Can the smallest change that would move a patient below a screening threshold be computed exactly? And can a narrative verifier catch errors about the model, not only errors about the data?');
p('To answer them we propose a framework built on the principle of *explanation by construction and verification by re-derivation*. Its first contribution is GAMA (Algorithm 1), a guideline-anchored monotone additive model. GAMA represents each continuous risk factor by a piecewise-linear shape function whose knots lie at guideline cut-points—the ACC/AHA 2017 and JNC 7 blood-pressure stages, the ATP III cholesterol categories, the ADA glycaemic thresholds and the WHO BMI classes. Its shape functions are non-decreasing for risk factors with an established adverse direction, extrapolate flatly outside the training range, and decompose the log-odds exactly into named, banded contributions, so no post-hoc explainer is needed. The second contribution is a certified narrative compiler and an independent certificate verifier (Algorithm 2). Each statement in a narrative carries a certificate stating the value, unit, guideline band, log-odds contribution, odds multiplier, rank and rule identifier. The verifier, which sees only the text, the record and the frozen model, re-derives all of these, including the magnitudes and rankings that a value-binding gate cannot test.');
p('The third contribution is an exact minimum-cost counterfactual search, referred to as EMC (Algorithm 3). Because GAMA is additive, the cheapest admissible change of at most two modifiable measurements that brings a patient below the screening threshold can be found by sorting and binary search, without a solver, and every solution is re-scored by the full model. The fourth contribution is a Mondrian conformal screening layer (Algorithm 4) whose coverage is guaranteed separately within each sex × age × outcome cell, so that low-confidence predictions are reported as indeterminate rather than hidden behind a single threshold. The final contribution is a leakage-free empirical evaluation on the public Framingham dataset. It compares GAMA with thirteen classifiers from eight model families and with three ablations for discrimination, calibration and net benefit, with paired bootstrap and Friedman–Nemenyi tests. It also benchmarks the real-time latency of the full pipeline on every test patient, and it measures explanation monotonicity and stability, counterfactual optimality, verifier sensitivity on 21 injected-error types with meaning-preserving controls, and subgroup coverage. All code and per-record outputs are released.');
p('The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 reviews related work and summarises it in a comparative table. Section 3 introduces the clinical and methodological background on which the framework builds. Section 4 presents the proposed system, and Section 5 gives its algorithms with their complexity. Section 6 describes the dataset and experimental design and reports and discusses the results. Section 7 concludes, and Section 8 outlines the future scope of the work. The principal notation is listed in the nomenclature table at the end of the paper.');

// ================= II. RELATED WORK =================
h1('2. Related Work');
p('Cardiovascular risk prediction has a long tradition of transparent statistical models. The Framingham general cardiovascular risk profile combined age, sex, cholesterol, blood pressure, smoking and diabetes in a Cox model that can be evaluated on paper {dagostino2008}, and the Pooled Cohort Equations extended this approach to multi-ethnic cohorts and an atherosclerotic endpoint {goff2014}. Both assume that risk factors act additively and approximately linearly on a transformed scale. Recent machine-learning studies on the Framingham dataset and related data have replaced these equations with hybrid ensembles, boosted trees and deep networks, typically reporting higher retrospective discrimination and attaching post-hoc explanations such as SHAP or LIME {talukder2025,adekoya2025,chaudhary2026,raman2026}. In these studies, explanation is an addition to a model chosen for accuracy, and the explanation is not used to constrain or verify the model.');
p('Interpretable additive models offer an alternative. Generalized additive models express the link-transformed outcome as a sum of univariate shape functions {hastie1986}. Lou et al. learned such shape functions by boosting and added selected pairwise interactions {lou2012}, and Caruana et al. showed on a pneumonia cohort that an intelligible additive model can expose clinically dangerous patterns—such as asthma appearing protective—that a black box would conceal {caruana2015}. The explainable boosting machine (EBM) implemented in InterpretML is the current reference implementation {nori2019}, and neural additive models learn each shape function with a small network {agarwal2021}. These models decompose predictions exactly, but their shape functions are learned freely. They may be jagged, non-monotone or counter-intuitive, and their bins do not coincide with the cut-points clinicians use. Monotonicity constraints address the first problem: lattice models enforce monotonicity exactly {gupta2016}, and gradient-boosting libraries such as LightGBM support per-feature monotone constraints {ke2017}. A monotone tree ensemble, however, must still be explained post hoc, and its explanation inherits the instability of the ensemble.');
p('Post-hoc explanation has been studied critically. Shapley-value attributions {lundberg2017} are exact for tree ensembles through TreeSHAP {lundberg2020}, but different value functions answer different questions, and with dependent features marginal and conditional Shapley values can differ substantially {aas2021}. LIME’s local surrogates depend on the sampling neighbourhood {ribeiro2016,salih2025}. Practitioners regularly observe that explainers disagree {krishna2022}, and Slack et al. built models whose biased behaviour SHAP and LIME failed to reveal {slack2020}. Surveys of explainable AI in health care stress that explanations must be faithful as well as understandable {markus2021}, and systematic evidence shows that explanations can either raise or lower clinician trust {rosenbacke2024}. Attempts to quantify trust from model confidence and explainer agreement {pal2026,bansal2025} summarise these signals but cannot by themselves establish that an explanation describes the model correctly.');
p('Counterfactual explanations describe the smallest change to the input that changes a model’s decision {wachter2018}. Ustun et al. computed optimal actionable recourse for linear classifiers with integer programming {ustun2019}, and DiCE generates diverse counterfactuals for arbitrary models by gradient-based or random search, without an optimality guarantee {mothilal2020}. Karimi et al. emphasised that a counterfactual describes the model, not the effect of an intervention in the world {karimi2021}, and Verma et al. review the field {verma2020}. On the uncertainty side, conformal prediction provides finite-sample coverage guarantees under exchangeability {vovk2005,angelopoulos2023}. Its Mondrian form guarantees coverage within each prespecified category {vovk2005}, and class-conditional set-valued classifiers guarantee coverage within each label {sadinle2019}, which matters in screening because marginal coverage can be achieved while the minority class is poorly covered.');
p('Finally, generative explanation has emerged as a separate line of work. Reviews of explainable generative AI propose using language models to convert structured model outputs into clinical summaries {kapadiya2026}, while the hallucination literature documents unsupported content in generated text {ji2023} and large clinical language models still require careful evaluation {singhal2023}. Our earlier triple-layer framework {paper1} verified generated narratives against a structured payload but, as explained in Section 1, could verify only statements about the data. Table {T:lit} summarises representative studies along the dimensions that motivate this work. No previous system combines an exact, clinically constrained and guideline-anchored model with verification of model-level statements, exact counterfactuals and subgroup-conditional uncertainty.');
