h2('6.10 Real-Time Feasibility');
const rs = (g, st) => f(RT[g][st].median, 1), r95 = (g, st) => f(RT[g][st].p95, 1);
p(`All ${RT.n.toLocaleString('en-US')} narratives produced in the real-time run passed certificate verification (${RT.passed.toLocaleString('en-US')} of ${RT.n.toLocaleString('en-US')}). Table {T:rt} and Fig. {F:rt} report the latency of each stage. For a screen-negative patient the complete pipeline—prediction, exact explanation, conformal status, narrative and verification—took a median of ${rs('negative', 'total')} ms (95th percentile ${r95('negative', 'total')} ms). For a screen-positive patient, the exact counterfactual search added a median of ${rs('positive', 'counterfactual')} ms, giving ${rs('positive', 'total')} ms in total (95th percentile ${r95('positive', 'total')} ms). Run sequentially on one core, the pipeline processed ${f(RT.throughput_per_s, 0)} patients per second on average. GAMA’s exact explanation took ${f(RT.explainers['GAMA exact contributions'].median, 1)} ms per record. That is slower than TreeSHAP (${f(RT.explainers['TreeSHAP (LightGBM)'].median, 1)} ms) and EBM term scores (${f(RT.explainers['EBM term scores'].median, 1)} ms), because of data-frame overhead in our implementation rather than the arithmetic. It is about ${f(RT.explainers['KernelSHAP (LightGBM, 500 samples)'].median / RT.explainers['GAMA exact contributions'].median, 0)} times faster than KernelSHAP and ${f(RT.explainers['LIME (LightGBM, 5,000 samples)'].median / RT.explainers['GAMA exact contributions'].median, 0)} times faster than LIME, the model-agnostic explainers that a black-box model would require. Every stage is therefore well within interactive limits.`);
const stN = { predict: 'Prediction', explain: 'Exact explanation', conformal: 'Conformal status', counterfactual: 'Counterfactual search (EMC)', compile: 'Narrative compilation', verify: 'Certificate verification', total: '**Total pipeline**' };
tbl({ label: 'rt', caption: 'Per-Patient Latency of the Proposed Pipeline (ms)', widths: [3038, 1100, 1100, 1100, 1100, 1100, 1100],
  headers: ['Stage', 'All: median', 'All: p95', 'Screen-positive: median', 'Screen-positive: p95', 'Screen-negative: median', 'Screen-negative: p95'],
  rows: Object.keys(stN).map(st => st === 'counterfactual' ? [stN[st], '—', '—', rs('positive', st), r95('positive', st), 'not run', 'not run'] : [stN[st], rs('all', st), r95('all', st), rs('positive', st), r95('positive', st), rs('negative', st), r95('negative', st)])
    .concat([['*Explanation only (comparison, 250 records)*', '', '', '', '', '', '']])
    .concat(Object.entries(RT.explainers).sort((a, b) => a[1].median - b[1].median).map(([m, v]) => [m, f(v.median, 1), f(v.p95, 1), '', '', '', ''])),
  note: `All: ${RT.n.toLocaleString('en-US')} complete-case test records (screen-positive ${RT.positive.n.toLocaleString('en-US')}, screen-negative ${RT.negative.n.toLocaleString('en-US')}), processed one at a time on one core of a 2.8 GHz Intel Xeon. p95, 95th percentile.` });
img('fig_rt.png', 0.95, 'rt');
cap('**Fig. {F:rt}.** (a) Median and 95th-percentile time of each pipeline stage for screen-positive records. (b) Median time per explanation for GAMA and for common explainers applied to LightGBM (log scale).');

h2('6.11 Discussion');
p('On this cohort, a model whose explanation is exact and whose shape agrees with clinical direction discriminated as well as the best of thirteen classifiers and significantly better than ten of them, including XGBoost and LightGBM. This is consistent with Rudin’s argument that, for tabular clinical data, the accuracy–interpretability trade-off is often illusory {rudin2019}, and with our earlier finding that the choice of model family hardly mattered on Framingham data {paper1}. With ROC-AUC around 0.72 for every family, the practical question is not which model is most accurate but which can be explained, checked and corrected. GAMA can be read band by band (Fig. {F:shapes}), its narrative cannot contradict it, and its constraints rule out the local reversals that affected up to 40% of glucose and BMI queries for EBM.');
p(`The thirteen-classifier benchmark adds two observations. First, the leading three systems—EBM, logistic regression and GAMA—are all additive in the log-odds, and every tree ensemble, kernel, neighbourhood and neural model ranked below them. On a small, imbalanced cohort with fifteen mostly monotone risk factors, flexibility appears to add variance rather than signal. Second, the real-time benchmark shows that certification is cheap. Verification adds a few milliseconds, and only the counterfactual search, run only for positive screens, takes tens of milliseconds. By contrast, the model-agnostic explainers that a black-box model would need, KernelSHAP and LIME, take one to two orders of magnitude longer per patient and still yield approximations.`);
p('The verification results show why certificates matter. A value–concept binding gate checks what a narrative says about the patient. More than 40% of the injected errors concerned what the narrative says about the model—how large a contribution is, which factor is strongest, what a change would do and how certain the screen is—and such a gate cannot check those statements by design. These are precisely the statements that make a narrative useful and that a reader is least able to verify. Certificates make them checkable by turning each one into an arithmetic identity over the frozen model. The approach depends on the model being additive: for a black-box model, the same statements would be certificates about an approximation rather than about the model.');
p(`EMC answers the question clinicians most often ask—what would have to change for this patient to fall below the threshold?—and guarantees that no cheaper answer exists on the admissible grid. Systolic blood pressure dominated the optimal changes, which agrees with the GAMA shape: it rises in every band, and systolic pressure varies widely enough that a modest change crosses a band. That ${pc(1 - CF.emc_found / CF.n, 0)}% of positive screens had no admissible counterfactual is informative in its own right, because their risk was driven by age and sex. As the narrative states and recourse research warns {karimi2021}, a model counterfactual is not the effect of treatment.`);
p('Finally, with modest discrimination, a 90% guarantee that holds in every subgroup and outcome class necessarily leaves most patients in the indeterminate band. We regard this as a correct description of the model rather than a defect of the method. Marginal conformal prediction appears better only because it fails silently for the patients a screen is meant to find; the Mondrian status makes the model’s limits explicit in every narrative.');
h2('6.12 Limitations');
p('The study has several limitations. All results come from one public teaching dataset with internal nested cross-validation, and external, temporal and prospective validation are required before any clinical use. Monotonicity is a modelling choice: constraining BMI removed a J-shaped association that may reflect illness-related weight loss, and the constraints encode priors that clinicians should review for each population. Proposition 2 guarantees faithfulness to the model within a closed grammar, not clinical correctness; the narratives are deliberately plain, and a free-text generator would need each sentence mapped back to a certificate. EMC is exact on a discrete grid with at most two changes and fixed healthy floors, and other cost functions, larger change sets or causal constraints would require extensions. The verifier challenge uses constructed errors of known types and does not estimate how often errors would arise from a particular generator. The dataset lacks HDL cholesterol, race, kidney function and fasting status, so guideline risk equations and glycaemic categories could not be assessed, and narratives for records with missing inputs were not evaluated. Finally, no human evaluation was performed, so it remains to be tested whether clinicians understand or act on certified narratives better than on other explanations.');

// ================= VII. CONCLUSION =================
h1('7. Conclusion');
p(`This paper presented an interpretable-by-design framework for ten-year coronary heart disease risk that replaces post-hoc explanation and free-text verification with four connected algorithms: a guideline-anchored monotone additive model, a certified narrative compiler and verifier, an exact minimum-cost counterfactual search and Mondrian conformal screening. The design principle is that every statement shown to a clinician—each contribution, its magnitude and rank, each guideline category, each suggested change and each uncertainty status—is derived from a single interpretable model and can be re-checked against it by a simple program.`);
p(`On the public Framingham dataset, under leakage-free repeated nested cross-validation, GAMA reached a ROC-AUC of ${f(T.GAMA.AUC)}, ranked third of fourteen systems, was statistically tied with the best, and significantly outperformed ten classifiers, including XGBoost and LightGBM. It produced no monotonicity violations and the most stable attributions. The counterfactual search was provably optimal and was never undercut by baseline searches. The verifier blocked every injected statement that was actually false, including the ${pc(1 - VT.bind / VT.n, 0)}% that a value-binding gate missed, with no false alarms on meaning-preserving rewrites. Conformal screening kept coverage near the nominal 90% in every sex, age and outcome subgroup, and the complete pipeline ran in tens of milliseconds per patient. These results show that, for tabular cardiovascular risk prediction, exact and verifiable explanation need not cost discrimination.`);

// ================= 8. FUTURE SCOPE =================
h1('8. Future Scope');
p('Several directions follow from this work. First, the framework should be validated externally and prospectively on contemporary cohorts that record the inputs the Framingham teaching dataset lacks—HDL cholesterol, kidney function, verified fasting glucose and ethnicity—so that guideline risk equations such as the Pooled Cohort Equations can be computed and compared with the model. Validation on Indian and other South Asian populations is a priority, because Western risk equations are known to transport poorly to them.');
p('Second, the certified narrative can be made more fluent without losing verifiability. A language model could paraphrase the compiled narrative under a grammar that maps each generated sentence back to its certificate, so that the verifier continues to check every claim. The same mechanism would support patient-facing and multilingual narratives, for example in Kannada and Hindi, with verification performed on a language-independent certificate.');
p('Third, the counterfactual search can be extended to larger change budgets by dynamic programming over discretised log-odds reductions, to clinician-specified cost functions, and to causal constraints that link, for instance, weight loss to blood-pressure change. Fourth, knot positions and monotonicity priors could be learned or adapted per population under clinician review, and the conformal layer extended to finer subgroups as larger cohorts become available.');
p('Finally, the clinical value of certified narratives must be tested with their intended users. A randomised reader study with physicians should measure comprehension, detection of erroneous model output and over-reliance, followed by a silent prospective deployment that monitors calibration drift, verifier block rates and latency before any clinical use.');

